    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d185">

                <pb facs="0347" n="231" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg231"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 893.512/62.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Minister Reinsch.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-12-20">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 20, 1915</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your 807, October 29. Department reiterates its views expressed in its No. 90,
                    May 4, 1914,<anchor n="67" xml:id="d185-f1"/> predicating its consent to stamp
                    tax upon assent by other foreign Governments, which apparently has been
                    refused.</p>
                <p>Inform interested Americans, however, they may think it best to view matter in
                    practical light of probable decision Chinese courts respecting unstamped
                    documents.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
